Phocinae decide — Android sample

This sample runs Phocinae-Largha-150M-v1 (the Phocinae project, Apache License 2.0;
https://huggingface.co/Phocinae/Phocinae-Largha-150M-v1, v1.1, model.safetensors sha256
b6472511…) as LiteRT graphs from https://huggingface.co/litert-community/Phocinae-Largha-150M-LiteRT.
The publisher's NOTICE is retained in licenses/Phocinae-NOTICE.txt.

The host side (Gemma-style BPE tokenizer with Metaspace and byte fallback, the typed-question
prompt builder, the marker decoder and the temperature lookup) is the Kotlin port written for the
Laya multilingual sample in this repository; its protocol follows Laya 0.3.4 by Convai Innovations
(Apache License 2.0, https://huggingface.co/convaiinnovations/laya), which Phocinae-Largha-150M-v1's
own runtime (phocinae-server) implements as well. That port is retained with its attribution in
licenses/Laya-APACHE-2.0.txt.

Model conversion changes: static windows (256 / 384 / 512 tokens), explicit attention and
rotary-position constants, LayerNorm computed on inputs scaled by a power of two (exact in fp32,
finite in fp16), a separate action head, host-side token embedding lookup from the checkpoint's
float16 rows, and FP16 storage of fully connected weights. The checkpoint tokenizer and
rl_agent_config.json (the publisher's temperatures) are used unchanged.

The encoder derives from mmBERT-small, declared MIT by its model card.
Attribution: Marc Marone, Orion Weller, William Fleshman, Eugene Yang, Dawn Lawrie, and
Benjamin Van Durme; Johns Hopkins Center for Language and Speech Processing and collaborators.
https://huggingface.co/jhu-clsp/mmBERT-small

The invented example states and the four question sets in presets.json are this sample's own;
the question shapes follow the typed-decisions protocol (LocalLLaMA/typed-decisions, Apache-2.0).
ModernBERT, Transformers and tokenizers license texts are retained under licenses/.
